1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,337
Welcome to Module Twenty-Four.

2
00:00:02,337 --> 00:00:06,837
If you have ever scored animal behaviour from video, you know this routine.

3
00:00:06,837 --> 00:00:11,900
Before anyone's scores count, two observers watch the same clips on their own.

4
00:00:11,900 --> 00:00:13,587
You compare their scores.

5
00:00:13,587 --> 00:00:15,137
You compute agreement.

6
00:00:15,137 --> 00:00:17,650
And whoever drifts gets retrained.

7
00:00:17,650 --> 00:00:22,612
Without that check, a rise in aggression might just mean a different person was watching.

8
00:00:22,612 --> 00:00:25,750
Yesterday your grader was a few lines of code.

9
00:00:25,750 --> 00:00:29,600
It compared a label with the right answer, and it can never drift.

10
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:32,525
But many outputs don't have one right form.

11
00:00:32,525 --> 00:00:33,562
A summary.

12
00:00:33,562 --> 00:00:35,700
An answer from your field notebook.

13
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:38,587
A short synthesis of three papers.

14
00:00:38,587 --> 00:00:44,250
For those you need an observer, and more and more often, that observer is another language model.

15
00:00:44,250 --> 00:00:45,750
We call it a judge.

16
00:00:45,750 --> 00:00:49,212
A judge is useful, and it is also a suspect.

17
00:00:49,212 --> 00:00:53,475
The research collected in this pack describes three classic biases.

18
00:00:53,475 --> 00:00:56,937
Judges tend to prefer whichever answer they read first.

19
00:00:56,937 --> 00:00:59,212
They tend to prefer longer answers.

20
00:00:59,212 --> 00:01:02,500
And they can recognise their own writing and favour it.

21
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:04,250
So the rules are simple.

22
00:01:04,250 --> 00:01:06,375
Use code whenever you can.

23
00:01:06,375 --> 00:01:18,400
When you need a judge, give it a rubric made of checkable claims, ask for pass or fail rather than a score out of five, use a different model from the one you are testing, and check it against your own judgement before you trust it.

24
00:01:18,400 --> 00:01:20,962
Run it twice on the same answer, too.

25
00:01:20,962 --> 00:01:23,750
If the verdict changes, that is noise.

26
00:01:23,750 --> 00:01:28,112
The second idea today is a diagnostic key for retrieval systems.

27
00:01:28,112 --> 00:01:31,687
Your field-notebook search can fail in two places.

28
00:01:31,687 --> 00:01:37,000
It can fetch the wrong evidence, or it can write an answer the evidence doesn't support.

29
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:44,387
Two metrics look at the fetching: did the retrieved passages contain what the answer needs, and were the useful ones ranked first?

30
00:01:44,387 --> 00:01:49,825
Two look at the writing: is every claim supported, and does the answer address the question?

31
00:01:49,825 --> 00:01:54,687
Read them together, like a two-by-two table, and you know which half to fix.

32
00:01:54,687 --> 00:01:57,037
The third idea is tracing.

33
00:01:57,037 --> 00:02:00,850
A run can finish without a single error and still be wrong.

34
00:02:00,850 --> 00:02:09,300
So you record everything: the question, the passages, the prompt, the answer, the verdicts, the time each step took.

35
00:02:09,300 --> 00:02:13,037
Then when something fails, you look at the path instead of guessing.

36
00:02:13,037 --> 00:02:18,037
In the lab, you'll write expected answers for your ten questions from Module Twenty.

37
00:02:18,037 --> 00:02:26,712
Your coding agent will run your search, generate answers, score them with a judge from a different model family, and save a trace for every question.

38
00:02:26,712 --> 00:02:31,950
Then you will label ten answers yourself, and measure how well the judge agrees with you.

39
00:02:31,950 --> 00:02:38,762
Start with Part A. Write your ten expected answers, in your own words, before you see anything the model says.

